From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 09:37:51 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 43/59] hjviewer: operator's view: the users and the PCs of the
 service, for its operator
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

hjv-api names its operators by portal ID (HJV_API_OPERATORS, set on the server
only; its privacy statement calls them 최고운영자, super operators). In an
account build the app gives such an account a read-only view of who uses
HJViewer and of the PCs logged in to an account: Settings → Account has
"최고운영자: 사용자와 PC" ("Super operator: users and PCs") for it, and for
nobody else. Nothing in the view acts on a
user or a PC.

The role (src/hjviewer_account/api.rs): the account module learns it from the
answers it already observes, before the app reads them, and keeps it in
LocalConfig "hjv-role", which main_get_common gives the screens:

- the login answer's user.hjv_role, with the token, as it takes the key
  serial; an answer without it is another account's;
- the key list's top-level hjv_role (GET /api/hjv/keys, 200), which the app
  asks when it starts and when it comes back to the front, so that a role the
  server gave or took back shows without a new login;
- the overview's 404, hjv-api's answer to an account that is no operator, as
  to an unknown path: the role goes (the 404 of any other path does not);
- an explicit logout, and a login dropped for want of its account key, forget
  it.

Only "operator" is a role. It only picks what the screens offer: hjv-api
checks it on every request of the view.

The screens are in hjviewer_admin.dart; their texts, the reading of the
answer, its order and the search are in hjviewer_admin_texts.dart, plain Dart
for ci.sh's web stand-in:

- the entry: a button under [로그인한 기기] in the desktop's account card (D7's
  panel), a tile above "로그인한 기기" on a phone (D8). It follows the login
  and the role as they change. For everybody else the phone's tile is empty,
  which settings_ui's iOS section draws nothing for between two tiles.
- the view: a dialog on a desktop, a page on a phone. GET
  /api/hjv/admin/overview goes as the account screens' requests do
  (hjvAccountGet: signed with the account key, a 401 ending the login). On
  top: "계정에 로그인한 PC만 보입니다. 아무도 로그인하지 않은 PC는 서버가 알지
  못합니다. IP는 PC가 마지막으로 서버와 동기화할 때 서버가 본 주소입니다. 이
  화면을 열거나 새로 고칠 때마다 운영자 기록에 남습니다.", a search and
  [새로 고침]; then "사용자 5명 · 활성 3명(최근 30일 사용) · PC 4대 · 10/14 09:30
  기준" and two tabs:
  - users: name and portal ID, 활성 or 비활성, the last use, the logged-in
    devices and the PCs; the active ones first, then by last use, newest
    first;
  - PCs: host name and PC ID, the account, the system (hjv-api's platform
    word: windows reads Windows, macos macOS, linux Linux, android Android,
    ios iOS, another word as it is) and HJViewer version, the last
    sync, the IP of the last sync and the LAN addresses of the PC's wake
    report, and its password-less state in words (켜짐, PC에서 꺼 둠, 관리자가
    일시 중지함, 서버와 맞추지 못함, PC가 아직 확인하지 않음, 다시 로그인 필요;
    another state as hjv-api names it); by account, then by host name, those
    without one last.
  Where the view is 720 wide or more each tab is a table, narrower a tile of
  lines per user or PC. Times show in the device's time zone, with the year
  when it is not the overview's. The part above the lists takes at most 60%
  of the view's height and scrolls in it when it is higher (a phone on its
  side with the keyboard up, a big font): the lists keep the rest, and
  nothing overflows. The search keeps, in both tabs, the rows that hold each
  of its words in any case; a user's state is a word that must be all of it
  (활성 finds the active users only, though it is in 비활성); a user's ID or
  name also finds the user's PCs, and the tabs then say how many of how many.
  [새로 고침] asks again, one request at a time; a failure says why and keeps
  what was shown, and the next answer takes the failure's words away. Only
  version 1 of the answer is read; another says to update the app.
- the view closes, takes its lists off the screen at once and asks nothing
  more:
  - at a 404, with "이 계정은 최고운영자가 아니어서 화면을 닫았습니다."; the
    entry goes, also when the view that asked was closed before the answer
    came (hjvAdminLoad has the entries look again after any 404 of the
    overview);
  - at a 401 of its request, and as soon as the login ends while it is open
    (gFFI.userModel.userName empty: a key logged out from another device,
    found by any request), with "로그인이 풀려서 화면을 닫았습니다.".
  The dialog closes once, however many of these come together.
- hjviewer_account.dart gets one-line hooks: the import, hjvAccountGet (its
  own request, for the view), the tile, the button, and after the key list
  hjvAdminRoleChanged, which has the entries read the role again.

Outside an account build none of this runs: the hooks are in the account
screens, which such a build does not show, and api.rs observes nothing.

Tests:
- src/hjviewer_account/api.rs: the_operators_entry_follows_the_role_hjv_api_gives
  (the login answer with and without the role and with other values, a
  second factor's question and a refused login, the key list's answers and
  what is not one, the overview's 404 and its other answers, the 404 of other
  paths, a logout, a dropped login).
- flutter/test/hjviewer_admin_test.dart (pure; for ci.sh's list): the entry's
  condition, reading the contract's answer and what is not version 1 or
  cannot be used, the 404 and the 401 that close the view and the other
  failures, the two orders, the search (by IP apart from the LAN, version,
  the user's state as a whole word in both languages), local times and the
  year, the rows of both layouts in Korean and English, a name that is the
  portal ID, the password-less states, no braces in any text.
- flutter/test/hjviewer_admin_widgets_test.dart (needs the generated bridge):
  the entry follows the login and the role, the phone's tile between two
  tiles on iOS and Android, the two tabs as a table and as tiles with nothing
  to press on a user or a PC and their text selectable, the search and
  [새로 고침] one at a time with a failure keeping the lists in the tab they
  were in and the next answer clearing it, empty lists and times read against
  the answer's own, a phone on its side with the keyboard up and big fonts
  without an overflow, a 404 taking the entry also after the view closed, a
  401 and the end of the login closing the view once and taking its lists, a
  close that throws leaving the logout to its other listeners, a view gone
  before its answer closing and failing nothing, the desktop dialog laying the
  table out and closing with [닫기] and Esc.

Known limits:
- A role given or taken back on the server shows at the app's next key list
  (its start, or back to the front after 30 seconds), or at once with the
  view's 404.
- The IP is what hjv-api saw: behind NAT, the address of the network's way
  out.
- On a phone on its side with the keyboard up the lists have 40% of a low
  screen while the search is typed; the part above them scrolls.
- The view learns that the login ended from the app's next account request
  (its own [새로 고침], the key list when the app comes back to the front):
  until one goes out, an open view stays as it is.

